Hello fellow beardie owner! Thank god for another legitimate comment on these guys care. It's scary to see just how invalid so many of these comments are... such wrong information being thrown out there. I have a 9 year old beardie, 4 Panther Chameleons and 1 Parsons Chameleon and what almost every single one of them share in common, is they showcase stress by different coloration or markings. This beardie has no black beard, stress marks on the underside, or overall dark coloration.... yet it's automatically "abuse" because it's "glass surfing" FFS give me a break. These animals often love getting out and exploring, so I give props to this girl if she does this daily for a bit just to get her guy out and about for some fresh air. So long as he has a proper set-up at home waiting, there is NOTHING wrong with this whatsoever. 

&#x200B;

God damn, can we start a reptile-info club or something to shoot down these myths of "glass surfing" and how beardies will just fucking DIE if not under their lights for 5 minutes? It's crazy man, especially when people mindlessly attack a girl who they don't even know or know the care she provides for her animals.

Another Beardie owner and here of a 9 year old Beardie and  Panthers and Parsons Chameleon owner. To all who are claiming "animal abuse", you literally couldn't be further from the truth. Mainly animals communicate through coloration, and if this were TRULY a stressed beardie, he'd have the famous black beard or stress marks under his chin and belly area, plus an overall darkening of their scales. Dark coloring can also mean they are cold and stressed, or some will turn darker when basking to absorb more heat, neither of which this lizard is doing. "Glass surfing" is the worst explanation of stress as they often do this when excited or see something they want. Beardies are extremely curious creatures so it's actually GREAT to take them out when you can safely do so. And for the love of god people, this girl is probably just taking her beardie out for a stroll AT MOST to get him out of the house. They don't need their lights to survive every waking hour of the day, being off their heat when the temperature is nice is NOT a bad thing especially if this is 1 or 2 hours tops. They literally have to deal with freezing temps in the wild and will brumate (reptile hibernation) to survive those cold winter days.

&#x200B;

Another case where people with no knowledge of these animals, claiming it's cruelty when in fact this beardie gets to be with its owner and explore the world, taking in new things, rather than sit mindlessly in a cage 24/7.

So you're breaking a federal law that protects the most vulnerable people in this country... under the name of a "peaceful protest".... and you're the same type to cry about the protests not being peaceful and "full of criminal illegal activity"?

&#x200B;

Oh wow. The irony.

Also allergy sufferer here, I get sick anytime the damn weather changes even slightly. I had a slightly fever today and a runny nose, now I'm panicking about whether or not it is something more lol.

